WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.717
The year is 1975.

00:00:02.182 --> 00:00:04.372
You have membranes from frog red blood cells.

00:00:04.967 --> 00:00:09.507
You switch the system on with isoproterenol, a
drug that acts like adrenaline.

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:13.879
Your only measurement is how much cyclic AMP the
membranes make.

00:00:14.617 --> 00:00:21.004
You know that a GTP-binding protein sits between
the receptor and the enzyme, adenylyl cyclase.

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:22.531
You do two runs.

00:00:23.224 --> 00:00:28.235
1. With ordinary GTP: switch the system on with
isoproterenol.

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:32.086
Then wash all of the isoproterenol away, and
keep measuring.

00:00:32.875 --> 00:00:36.847
2. The same run, but with a synthetic analogue
of GTP.

00:00:37.522 --> 00:00:40.595
The protein can bind this analogue, but it
cannot break it.

00:00:41.179 --> 00:00:43.043
Write your predictions before you start.

00:00:43.832 --> 00:00:44.987
Answer two questions.

00:00:45.784 --> 00:00:50.799
1. What does cyclic AMP production do after the
wash, in each run?

00:00:51.621 --> 00:00:56.712
2. What does the comparison tell you about what
sets how long one activation lasts?

00:00:57.074 --> 00:01:00.339
Schramm and Rodbell did this experiment in 1975.

00:01:00.874 --> 00:01:11.299
They gave frog red blood cell membranes
isoproterenol together with Gpp(NH)p, a GTP
analogue that the protein can bind but cannot
cut.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:15.071
Adenylyl cyclase switched on and stayed on.

00:01:15.635 --> 00:01:24.006
It stayed on after extensive washing, after
sonication (breaking the membranes with sound
waves), and after treatment with detergent.

00:01:24.896 --> 00:01:29.085
Propranolol, which blocks the receptor, could no
longer reverse it.

00:01:30.035 --> 00:01:33.812
With isoproterenol alone, the enzyme did not
stay on.

00:01:34.488 --> 00:01:39.165
When the isoproterenol was diluted away,
activity fell to nearly nothing.

00:01:40.036 --> 00:01:42.914
Breitwieser and Szabo saw the same effect in
heart cells.

00:01:43.487 --> 00:01:50.637
There, GTP analogues that cannot be cut opened
the atrial potassium channel with no agonist
present at all.

00:01:51.104 --> 00:01:52.676
Here is how the G protein works.

00:01:53.334 --> 00:01:55.844
1. A hormone binds a receptor in the membrane.

00:01:56.369 --> 00:01:58.618
2. The occupied receptor does one thing.

00:01:59.103 --> 00:02:05.395
It removes a used GDP molecule from a nearby G
protein, and a new GTP molecule takes its place.

00:02:06.031 --> 00:02:09.536
3. GTP has three phosphate groups in a row.

00:02:10.034 --> 00:02:16.504
When the G protein holds GTP, it changes shape,
and this shape switches on adenylyl cyclase.

00:02:17.121 --> 00:02:20.348
4. The G protein is also a weak enzyme.

00:02:20.897 --> 00:02:22.962
It acts on the GTP that it holds.

00:02:23.446 --> 00:02:25.652
Sooner or later it cuts off the third phosphate.

00:02:26.308 --> 00:02:30.260
This leaves GDP, and the G protein returns to
its off shape.

00:02:31.134 --> 00:02:33.645
5. The G protein cannot reload itself.

00:02:34.403 --> 00:02:39.332
Another occupied receptor must remove the GDP
before a new GTP can bind.

00:02:39.919 --> 00:02:45.830
So the time a cell keeps responding to
adrenaline is set by how slowly the G protein
cuts GTP.

00:02:46.614 --> 00:02:48.640
The G protein works as its own timer.

00:02:49.093 --> 00:02:50.800
It also moves freely in the membrane.

00:02:51.332 --> 00:02:59.357
So one occupied receptor can switch on many G
protein molecules, one after another, and each
one can switch on its own adenylyl cyclase.
